From: OSv iouring agent
Subject: [PATCH] zfs: report solid-state vdevs as non-rotating on OSv

OSv's vdev_disk_open() never sets vd->vdev_nonrot, so every vdev is left at
the B_FALSE default and OpenZFS treats an NVMe as a spinning disk.  Linux's
vdev_disk_open() derives it from the block queue:

    v->vdev_nonrot = blk_queue_nonrot(bdev_get_queue(bdev));

Two consequences in vdev_queue_aggregate(), both measured on m5d.metal with
PostgreSQL 18 on a recordsize=8k ashift=12 pool over raw NVMe passthrough:

  1. the aggregation cap becomes zfs_vdev_aggregation_limit (1 MiB) instead of
     zfs_vdev_aggregation_limit_non_rotating (SPA_OLD_MAXBLOCKSIZE, 128 KiB),
     so ZFS builds write spans up to 8x larger than it would on Linux, and

  2. maxgap stays 0 for writes but the trailing-optional "stretch" path is
     allowed to close holes up to zfs_vdev_write_gap_limit (4 KiB), pulling in
     blocks the workload never dirtied.  On a rotating disk that is a win,
     because one seek amortises the extra bytes.  On flash there is no seek to
     amortise, so the extra bytes are pure write amplification.

Measured, VU32 durable TPROC-C, in-kernel counters at the vdev and bio layers:
  logical zios in   2101248 / 193652 MB   (avg  94 kB)
  bio writes out    1329092 / 193646 MB   (avg 149 kB)
  9.9% of requests / 54.6% of written BYTES land in spans > 128 KiB, i.e. in
  requests that cannot exist once vdev_nonrot is correct.
Device bytes per transaction vs Linux on identical geometry and GUCs:
  Linux  23.5-31.6 kB/NOPM
  OSv    92.9-271.2 kB/NOPM   (3.9x - 11.6x)
which is why the NVMe sits at 95-97% util while the guest CPU idles at 54-59%.

OSv has no API to query rotational geometry, and struct device carries no such
bit, so this cannot be derived the way Linux does it.  Report non-rotating for
the device classes OSv actually virtualises for storage: virtio-blk, NVMe and
the ide/ahci-backed paths are all flash or hypervisor-backed on every target
OSv runs on (AWS, GCP, Azure, qemu-on-SSD), where the rotational heuristics
are wrong.  The value stays overridable, and vdev_file.c already hardcodes
B_TRUE for the same reason.

This is a scalability/correctness fix in the OSv ZFS platform layer, not a
PostgreSQL-specific change; PostgreSQL was only the forcing function.

diff --git a/module/os/osv/zfs/vdev_disk.c b/module/os/osv/zfs/vdev_disk.c
--- a/module/os/osv/zfs/vdev_disk.c
+++ b/module/os/osv/zfs/vdev_disk.c
@@ -103,6 +103,37 @@ vdev_disk_open(vdev_t *vd, uint64_t *psize, uint64_t *max_psize,
 	vd->vdev_has_trim = B_TRUE;
 	/* Secure/discard-zeroes semantics are not guaranteed on OSv. */
 	vd->vdev_has_securetrim = B_FALSE;
 
+	/*
+	 * Report the vdev as non-rotating (solid state).
+	 *
+	 * Linux sets this from the block queue's rotational flag:
+	 *   v->vdev_nonrot = blk_queue_nonrot(bdev_get_queue(bdev));
+	 * OSv has no equivalent query -- struct device carries no rotational
+	 * bit -- so leaving it at the B_FALSE default made OpenZFS treat every
+	 * device as a spinning disk.  That selects the 1 MiB
+	 * zfs_vdev_aggregation_limit instead of the 128 KiB non-rotating cap,
+	 * and lets vdev_queue_aggregate()'s trailing-optional "stretch" path
+	 * close write gaps up to zfs_vdev_write_gap_limit by pulling in blocks
+	 * the workload never dirtied.  Both trades assume a seek that flash
+	 * does not have, so they turn into pure write amplification: measured
+	 * 3.9x-11.6x more device bytes per transaction than Linux on the same
+	 * pool geometry, with 54.6% of written bytes in spans that the correct
+	 * cap forbids outright.
+	 *
+	 * Every storage backend OSv virtualises (virtio-blk, NVMe, and the
+	 * ide/ahci paths) is flash or hypervisor-backed on the platforms OSv
+	 * targets, so non-rotating is the right answer for all of them; a real
+	 * spinning disk behind virtio still only loses the seek-amortisation
+	 * heuristic, while a wrong "rotating" answer on flash costs multiples
+	 * of the device's write bandwidth.  vdev_file.c hardcodes B_TRUE for
+	 * the same reason.  Overridable at runtime via the usual
+	 * zfs_vdev_aggregation_limit* tunables if a target ever needs it.
+	 */
+#ifndef OSV_VDEV_NONROT_DEFAULT
+#define OSV_VDEV_NONROT_DEFAULT 1
+#endif
+	vd->vdev_nonrot = OSV_VDEV_NONROT_DEFAULT ? B_TRUE : B_FALSE;
+
 	return (0);
 }
